      <h1 class="visually-hidden">litmus</h1>
    </div>
    <p>Litmus is the first general-purpose open-source AI model for
    malware detection. Built for supply-chain security, it classifies
    threats from FreeBSD ELF binaries to Python libraries to VS Code
    extensions — anything <a href="/cleave/">cleave</a> can decompose,
    across 20+ languages and six binary formats.</p>
<p>Dial in your paranoia with flags like <code>--threshold-hostile</code>, or plug in
your own trained model to match your threat model.</p>

    <h2>Capabilities</h2>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li><strong>File scanning</strong> — scan files, directories, and archives against a local <a href="https://codeberg.org/atomdrift/litmus-models/" target="_blank">ML model</a></li>
      <li><strong>Process scanning</strong> — scan running processes (just the files underneath them, for now...)</li>
    </ul>

    Coming soon is advanced differential analysis - critical for the supply-chain analysis use case.

    <h2>Install</h2>

Homebrew (macOS or Linux):

<pre class="install-block">
brew tap atomdrift/tap https://codeberg.org/atomdrift/homebrew-tap.git

brew install atomdrift/tap/litmus
</pre>

From source

<pre class="install-block">
git clone --depth 1 https://codeberg.org/atomdrift/litmus.git
make install
</pre>

    <h2>Usage</h2>
    <pre class="install-block">$ litmus scan &lt;path&gt;</pre>

    <p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 0.8125rem;">For more thorough results, install
    <a href="https://github.com/rizinorg/rizin" target="_blank">Rizin</a> (binary reverse-engineering) and
    <a href="https://github.com/upx/upx" target="_blank">UPX</a> (unpacker).</p>

